Exactly. The Minotaur should be optional and user-shaped.

If someone wants challenge, it can be suspense, friction, a puzzle, an adversary, or uncertainty. If they want calm, it can disappear entirely, become a guide, a joke, a companion, or just a distant sound in the maze.

That gives the experience a better control surface:

intensity, ambiguity, threat, surprise, companionship, pace, and visibility can all be tuned.

So the maze stops being “the system decides what you endure” and becomes:

the system offers a world; the person chooses how that world meets them.

That is much closer to the humane version of adaptive immersion we’ve been circling.